/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 134 0 R
/T (Software teams today face a fundamental choice in how they build applications: the long-established practice of Traditional Coding, or the newer, faster paradigm of Low-Code Development using platforms such as Zoho Creator, Microsoft Power Apps, Mendix, and OutSystems.  This document presents a detailed comparative study of the two approaches. Traditional coding gives development teams complete control, unlimited customization, and strong performance, but requires significant time, cost, and specialist skill. Low-code development, exemplified by AI-assisted platforms like Zoho Creator, allows applications to be assembled visually through drag-and-drop components, cutting development time from weeks to hours while remaining accessible to non-programmers.  The report walks through the objectives of the study, the functional and non-functional requirements expected of each approach, a side-by-side architecture and workflow comparison, a representative implementation use case \(an Employee Management / Attendance application\), and the results and conclusions drawn from this comparison. The overall finding is that the two approaches are complementary rather than competing: low-code is best suited to rapid, standard business applications, while traditional coding remains essential for complex, performance-critical, and highly customized systems. )
/E (Software teams today face a fundamental choice in how they build applications: the long-established practice of Traditional Coding, or the newer, faster paradigm of Low-Code Development using platforms such as Zoho Creator, Microsoft Power Apps, Mendix, and OutSystems.  This document presents a detailed comparative study of the two approaches. Traditional coding gives development teams complete control, unlimited customization, and strong performance, but requires significant time, cost, and specialist skill. Low-code development, exemplified by AI-assisted platforms like Zoho Creator, allows applications to be assembled visually through drag-and-drop components, cutting development time from weeks to hours while remaining accessible to non-programmers.  The report walks through the objectives of the study, the functional and non-functional requirements expected of each approach, a side-by-side architecture and workflow comparison, a representative implementation use case \(an Employee Management / Attendance application\), and the results and conclusions drawn from this comparison. The overall finding is that the two approaches are complementary rather than competing: low-code is best suited to rapid, standard business applications, while traditional coding remains essential for complex, performance-critical, and highly customized systems. )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
